A||||9||Spektyr||BIG number 6 spark problem...||||4||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-26-2001||06:01 PM||||Okay, I may be a scrawny-looking guy, but I can turn a mean wrench.  I'm changing the spark plugs on my 85GT (along with a lot of other things).  The back ones are rusted in pretty badly, but good old fashioned blood, sweat, tears and grunting has yanked 2 of the 3 out of the block.<P>However... number 6 is being a real pain.  It seems the socked can't get on far enough to hit the hex on it.  I stuck a little mirror down there, but it's damn dark in there.  Even tried a mirror-flashlight combo but that didn't help.  I tried snapping the tip off in frustration at that point, just broke the ceramic and the core held together.<P>I'm more than a bit ticked off at this point, so I'm at the computer instead of the Fiero.  Anyone have any ideas?<P>Also, I got the Splitfire dual core spark plug wires.  The sparkplug boots on them look like the original boots, but I can't seem to get the metal heat shields all the way on them.  Is there a trick to this?  Do I need the heat shields?<P>Finally, I'm replacing the thermostat.  According to Haynes, you just twist off the thermostat housing cap and pull the thermo out.  I pulled on it as hard as I dared, but it didn't budge.  What's the deal?<P>Thanks in advance for the help.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||4
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||04-26-2001||06:26 PM||||There is more that likely debris around the spark plug. If you have access to compressed air, blow out around it. <BR>(wear eye protection!) If not take something and route out around it to clear use a long tube and use a vaccumn cleaner to clean it out.<P>It's a great idea to use the heat shields to protect your NEW wires, after all that's what they are there for. Try lubing them up to get them on the boots.<P>I take it you've got the cap off? Try wriggling the stat out back and forth. If that doesn't work, PULL HARDER! It's not a good idea to prise on the lip af the housing, but if you can locate a point off the housing to use as a fulcrum point you can try that.<P>Hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektyr||04-26-2001||06:38 PM||||Thanks I'll give the air a try.  I'll probably go buy some canned air.  I think maybe the car's just trying to decide if it likes me or not, making it hard for me to take care of it.  If I keep going and win the day then she'll decide I'm a good owner.<BR>Then again, maybe she just wants more blood.<P>hehe.<P>As for the thermo, I'm hoping to get it out without bending the top part, since it makes for a good winter-time thermostat.  (Just in case for some psycho reason I decide to drive it in the winter.)<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||04-26-2001||07:22 PM||||you may need to take off you deck lid and use a mirrow to see around the plug. I had the same problem recently. I used a dental cleaning tool to dislode the rust (any fine pointed tool would work) that had built up around the plug. there is very little clearance between the head and the hex on the plug. This stuff was pretty solid and had to be loosened before the air would do any good.<BR>Spray abit of blaster, WD40, or such around the plug that may help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-26-2001||09:53 PM||||Hehe, that's pretty much exactly what I had done.  I had the deck off when I posted before, had chiseled around with a very small screwdriver.  I bought a can if compressed air, stuck the nozzle down in there, hit the valve and blew a huge cloud of crud everywhere.  Sparkplug socket dropped right into place.  Like a bonehead I failed to do this on the other two back ones (were completed before I had the canned air).<P>The start-up exhaust was... ugly.  But it's running smoothly now.  About to road test it.  Today I installed...<P>Accel Coil<BR>Accel Ignition Module<BR>Bosch's top grade Distributer cap (unplanned)<BR>Bosch's top grade rotor (unplanned)<BR>Splitfire Sparkplugs and Wires.<BR>160 degree Thermostat<BR>176 degree Fan switch<BR>Bosch's top grade oil filter<BR>4 quarts of 10w-40<P>Only took about 8 hours with 3 trips to the Part store and one to Radio shack (for canned air and electrical connectors for the Fan switch).<P>I'm tired.  Very tired.<P>But tomorrow I'm rebuilding the headlight motors and getting rid of the Cat.<P>Must... go... faster...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||6
Z||000005||DavidHenshaw||04-26-2001||10:48 PM||||How can you pass inspection without a CAT? Just asking.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||fyerstarter||04-26-2001||10:58 PM||||Get a piece of straight pipe or a glasspack with two flanges welded on each end at the muffler shop. Have them do the same to your cat. Then when it comes inspection time just unbolt your straight pipe and slap in the cat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Spektyr||04-26-2001||11:25 PM||||Well, there's no emissions testing in Kansas.<P>:P<P>If I get lucky enough to move out of the land of flat, straight roads to a place that requires emissions, I can always just bolt in a cat long enough to pass, then bolt the pipe back in.<P>When was the last time an officer pulled you over and said "While I've got you here, let's check your catalytic converter"?<P>hehe<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000008||RacinRob||04-27-2001||01:32 AM||||on the note of a cop checking for a cat...  It's been done to two of my friends and my brother.  I personally think that it would be worth it to see the cop try and crawl under my car to see it...||||reg||1
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||04-27-2001||02:19 AM||||thats why I love kansas, no emissions testing.  heres a funny story, back in high school, just after I had fired up my firebird for the first time with the new engine, with open headers, a cop walked into the auto shop at the school, because of a fight earlier that day in the shop class.  This car, open headers, inside a metal building, made for alot of racket.  The cop walked out of the office and said to me "Im going to have to give you a ticket for loud mufflers"  I told him "officer you cant do that"  He said "why not?"  I said " because this car doesnt have any mufflers"  He just laughed and left.  anyway, cops are pretty cool here concerning loud cars, loud stereos, and that type of stuff.  ||||reg||1
